1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,787
Chapter 61: Endocrine Disorders.

3
00:00:05,787 --> 00:00:27,312
Thyroid Physiology And Pregnancy Hyperthyroidism Hypothyroidism Subclinical Hypothyroidism Iodine Deficiency Congenital Hypothyroidism Postpartum Thyroiditis Nodular Thyroid Disease Parathyroid Disease Adrenal Gland Disorders Pituitary Disorders References Endocrinopathies are closely related to pregnancy for several reasons.

4
00:00:27,312 --> 00:00:36,475
One example is the gestational proclivity for prodigious hormone secretion, which is perhaps best illustrated by placental lactogen in diabetes.

5
00:00:36,475 --> 00:00:42,850
This is the most common endocrinopathy encountered in pregnancy and discussed in Chapter 57.

6
00:00:42,850 --> 00:00:50,212
Pregnancy is also interrelated with some endocrinopathies that are at least partially due to autoimmune dysregulation.

7
00:00:50,212 --> 00:01:01,962
Clinical manifestations result from complex interplay among genetic, environmental, and endogenous factors that activate the immune system against targeted cells within endocrine organs.

8
00:01:01,962 --> 00:01:10,325
In one extraordinary interaction, studies have implicated maternal organ engraftment by fetal cells transferred during pregnancy.

9
00:01:10,325 --> 00:01:17,262
These cells later provoke antibody production, tissue destruction, and autoimmune endocrinopathies.

10
00:01:17,262 --> 00:01:19,375
Thyroid Disorders.

11
00:01:19,375 --> 00:01:26,062
Taken in aggregate, these disorders are common in young women and thus frequently encountered in pregnancy.

12
00:01:26,062 --> 00:01:33,987
Maternal and fetal thyroid function are intimately related, and drugs that affect the maternal thyroid affect the fetal gland.

13
00:01:33,987 --> 00:01:40,862
Moreover, thyroid autoantibodies are associated with increased rates of early pregnancy wastage.

14
00:01:40,862 --> 00:01:48,437
Uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis and untreated hypothyroidism are both associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes.

15
00:01:48,437 --> 00:01:58,325
Last, evidence suggests that the severity of some autoimmune thyroid disorders may be ameliorated during pregnancy, only to be exacerbated postpartum.

16
00:01:58,325 --> 00:02:01,937
# Thyroid Physiology and Pregnancy.

17
00:02:01,937 --> 00:02:11,225
Maternal thyroid changes are substantial, and normally altered gland structure and function can sometimes be confused with thyroid abnormalities.

18
00:02:11,225 --> 00:02:22,000
These alterations are discussed in detail in Chapter 4 (p. 71), and normal serum hormone level values are found in the Appendix (p. 1230).

19
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:30,837
First, maternal serum concentrations of thyroid binding globulin are increased concomitantly with total or bound thyroid hormone levels.

20
00:02:30,837 --> 00:02:43,462
Second, thyrotropin, also called thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), plays a central role in screening and diagnosis of many thyroid disorders, but levels change throughout pregnancy.

21
00:02:43,462 --> 00:02:55,262
Notably, TSH receptors are cross stimulated, albeit weakly, by massive quantities of human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) secreted by placental trophoblast.

22
00:02:55,262 --> 00:03:00,975
Because TSH does not cross the placenta, it has no direct fetal effects.

23
00:03:00,975 --> 00:03:09,750
During the first 12 weeks of gestation, when maternal hCG serum levels are maximal, thyroid hormone secretion is stimulated.

24
00:03:09,750 --> 00:03:22,462
The resulting greater serum free thyroxine (T4) levels act to suppress hypothalamic thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) and in turn limit pituitary TSH secretion .

25
00:03:22,462 --> 00:03:26,975
Accordingly, TRH is undetectable in maternal serum.

26
00:03:26,975 --> 00:03:35,687
Conversely, in fetal serum, beginning at midpregnancy, TRH becomes detectable, but levels are static and do not rise.

27
00:03:35,687 --> 00:03:38,325
Figure 61-1.

28
00:03:38,325 --> 00:03:49,900
Gestational-age-specific values for serum thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) levels (black lines) and free thyroxine (T4) levels (blue lines).

29
00:03:49,900 --> 00:03:55,925
Data were derived from 17,298 women tested during pregnancy.

30
00:03:55,925 --> 00:04:08,925
For each color, the dark solid lines represent the 50th percentile, whereas the upper and lower light lines represent the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles, respectively.

31
00:04:08,925 --> 00:04:13,887
Data from Casey, 2005; Dashe, 2005.

32
00:04:13,887 --> 00:04:18,675
Throughout pregnancy, maternal thyroxine is transferred to the fetus .

33
00:04:18,675 --> 00:04:31,175
Maternal thyroxine is important for normal fetal brain development, especially before the onset of fetal thyroid gland function (Bernal, 2007; Korevaar, 2016b).

34
00:04:31,175 --> 00:04:39,562
Both high and low maternal thyroid function in the first trimester are negatively associated with cortical gray matter volume in childhood .

35
00:04:39,562 --> 00:04:50,125
Although the fetal gland begins concentrating iodine and synthesizing thyroid hormone after 12 weeks’ gestation, maternal thyroxine contribution remains important.

36
00:04:50,125 --> 00:04:53,425
Maternal sources account for 30 percent

37
00:04:53,596 --> 00:04:56,783
of thyroxine in fetal serum at term .

38
00:04:56,733 --> 00:05:09,608
Still, developmental risks associated with maternal hypothyroidism after midpregnancy remain poorly understood (Morreale de Escobar, 2004; Sarkhail, 2016).

39
00:05:09,608 --> 00:05:13,046
# Autoimmunity and Thyroid Disease.

40
00:05:13,046 --> 00:05:20,471
Most thyroid disorders are inextricably linked to autoantibodies against nearly 200 thyrocyte components.

41
00:05:20,471 --> 00:05:29,958
These antibodies variably stimulate thyroid function, block function, or cause thyroid inflammation that may lead to follicular cell destruction.

42
00:05:29,958 --> 00:05:32,858
Often, these effects overlap.

43
00:05:32,858 --> 00:05:45,458
Thyroid-stimulating autoantibodies, also called thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSIs), bind and activate the TSH receptor to cause thyroid hyperfunction and growth.

44
00:05:45,458 --> 00:05:56,096
Although these antibodies are identified in most patients with classic Graves disease, simultaneous production of thyroidstimulating blocking antibodies may blunt this effect .

45
00:05:56,096 --> 00:06:03,821
Thyroid peroxidase (TPO) is a thyroid gland enzyme that normally functions to produce thyroid hormones.

46
00:06:03,821 --> 00:06:18,321
Thyroid peroxidase antibodies are directed against TPO and are identified in 5 to 15 percent of all gravidas (Abbassi-Ghanavati, 2010; Casey, 2007; Sarkhail, 2016).

47
00:06:18,321 --> 00:06:33,321
TPO antibodies have been associated in some studies with early pregnancy loss and preterm birth (Consortium on Thyroid and Pregnancy, 2019; Plowden, 2017; Thangaratinam, 2011).

48
00:06:33,321 --> 00:06:41,508
In another study with more than 1000 pregnant women with TPO antibodies, the risk for preterm birth was not elevated.

49
00:06:41,508 --> 00:06:44,683
The risk for placental abruption was greater .

50
00:06:44,683 --> 00:06:56,596
These women are also at high risk for postpartum thyroid dysfunction and at lifelong risk for permanent thyroid failure (Andersen, 2016; Jameson, 2015).

51
00:06:56,596 --> 00:06:59,233
Figure 61-2.

52
00:06:59,233 --> 00:07:20,146
Incidence of antithyroid peroxidase antibodies in 16,407 women who are normal or euthyroid, in 233 with isolated maternal hypothyroxinemia (IMH), in 598 with subclinical hypothyroidism (SCH), and in 134 with overt hypothyroidism.

53
00:07:20,146 --> 00:07:22,983
# Fetal Microchimerism.

54
00:07:22,983 --> 00:07:27,371
Autoimmune thyroid disease is more common in women than in men.

55
00:07:27,371 --> 00:07:32,883
One intriguing explanation for this disparity is fetal-to-maternal cell trafficking .

56
00:07:32,883 --> 00:07:37,508
Fetal cells are known to enter maternal circulation during pregnancy.

57
00:07:37,508 --> 00:07:43,383
When fetal lymphocytes enter maternal circulation, they can live for more than 20 years.

58
00:07:43,383 --> 00:07:50,471
Stem cell interchange can lead to engraftment in several maternal tissues and is termed fetal microchimerism.

59
00:07:50,471 --> 00:07:58,221
In some cases, this may involve the thyroid gland (Bianchi, 2003; Boddy, 2015).

60
00:07:58,221 --> 00:08:11,521
A high prevalence of Y chromosome–positive cells has been identified in the thyroid glands of women with Hashimoto thyroiditis—60 percent, or with Graves disease—40 percent (Renné, 2004).

61
00:08:11,521 --> 00:08:20,796
In another study of women giving birth to a male fetus, more circulating male mononuclear cells were found in those with Hashimoto thyroiditis .

62
00:08:20,796 --> 00:08:28,133
Conversely, for some autoimmune thyroid disorders, fetal microchimerism may actually have a protective role .

63
00:08:28,133 --> 00:08:30,783
# Hyperthyroidism.

64
00:08:30,783 --> 00:08:44,196
The incidence of thyrotoxicosis or hyperthyroidism in pregnancy varies and complicates between 0.4 and 1.7 percent of births when gestational-ageappropriate TSH threshold values are used .

65
00:08:44,196 --> 00:08:54,083
Because normal pregnancy simulates some clinical findings similar to thyroxine excess, clinically mild thyrotoxicosis may be difficult to diagnose.

66
00:08:54,083 --> 00:09:05,583
Suggestive findings include tachycardia that exceeds that usually seen with normal pregnancy, thyromegaly, exophthalmos, and failure to gain weight despite adequate food intake.

67
00:09:05,583 --> 00:09:08,771
Laboratory testing is confirmatory.

68
00:09:08,771 --> 00:09:15,933
TSH levels are markedly depressed, while serum free T4 (fT4) levels are elevated.

69
00:09:15,933 --> 00:09:25,371
Rarely, hyperthyroidism is caused by abnormally high serum triiodothyronine (T3) levels and called T3-toxicosis.

70
00:09:25,371 --> 00:09:27,996
Table 61-1.

71
00:09:27,996 --> 00:09:44,571
Incidence of Overt Hyperthyroidism in Pregnancy Thyrotoxicosis and Pregnancy The overwhelming cause of thyrotoxicosis in pregnancy is Graves disease, an organ-specific autoimmune process associated with thyroid-stimulating TSH-receptor antibodies .

72
00:09:44,571 --> 00:10:01,608
These antibodies are specific for Graves hyperthyroidism, and targeted assays have been proposed for diagnosis, management, and prognosis in pregnancies complicated by hyperthyroidism (Alexander, 2017; Barbesino, 2013).

73
00:10:01,608 --> 00:10:03,033
With

74
00:10:03,196 --> 00:10:11,433
Graves disease, during the course of pregnancy, hyperthyroid symptoms may initially worsen because of hCG stimulation.

75
00:10:11,383 --> 00:10:21,746
Symptoms then diminish after a decline in receptor-antibody titers in the second half of pregnancy (Mestman, 2012; Sarkhail, 2016).

76
00:10:21,746 --> 00:10:26,183
Levels of blocking antibodies also decline during pregnancy .

77
00:10:26,183 --> 00:10:32,571
Treatment Thyrotoxicosis during pregnancy can nearly always be controlled by thionamide drugs.

78
00:10:32,571 --> 00:10:43,946
Propylthiouracil (PTU) has been historically preferred because it partially inhibits the conversion of T4 to T3 and crosses the placenta less readily than methimazole.

79
00:10:43,946 --> 00:10:55,208
The latter has also been associated with a rare methimazole embryopathy, characterized by esophageal or choanal atresia and aplasia cutis, a congenital skin defect.

80
00:10:55,208 --> 00:11:12,921
In one analysis of Japanese women with first-trimester hyperthyroidism, the risk of major fetal malformation in pregnancies exposed to methimazole was twofold higher than risks with either PTU or potassium iodide use (Yoshihara, 2012, 2015).

81
00:11:12,921 --> 00:11:22,246
Specifically, seven of nine cases with aplasia cutis and the only case of esophageal atresia were in the group of methimazole-exposed fetuses.

82
00:11:22,246 --> 00:11:26,696
A PTU-associated embryopathy also has been described .

83
00:11:26,696 --> 00:11:31,546
Treatment with PTU is associated with a higher risk for liver injury .

84
00:11:31,546 --> 00:11:39,783
In 2009, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration issued a safety alert on PTU-associated hepatotoxicity.

85
00:11:39,783 --> 00:11:55,771
This warning initially prompted the American Thyroid Association and the American Association of Clinical Endocrinologists (2011) to recommend PTU therapy during the first trimester followed by a switch to methimazole beginning in the second trimester.

86
00:11:55,771 --> 00:12:01,083
The obvious disadvantage is that this might lead to poorly controlled thyroid function.

87
00:12:01,083 --> 00:12:18,746
More recently, the American Thyroid Association recommends PTU therapy through 16 weeks’ gestation but does not make a recommendation regarding switching to methimazole treatment because both agents are associated with adverse effects and the harm from poorer control during the switch is unknown .

88
00:12:18,746 --> 00:12:27,733
Transient leukopenia can be documented in up to 10 percent of women taking antithyroid drugs, but this does not require therapy cessation .

89
00:12:27,733 --> 00:12:36,096
In approximately 0.3 percent, however, agranulocytosis develops suddenly and mandates drug discontinuance .

90
00:12:36,096 --> 00:12:43,921
It is not dose related, and because of its acute onset, routine serial leukocyte counts during therapy are not helpful.

91
00:12:43,921 --> 00:12:52,821
Instead, if fever or sore throat develops, women are instructed to discontinue medication immediately and report for a complete blood count.

92
00:12:52,821 --> 00:12:55,758
Therapy may have other side effects.

93
00:12:55,758 --> 00:13:02,833
First, as noted, hepatotoxicity develops in approximately 0.1 percent of treated women.

94
00:13:02,833 --> 00:13:09,758
Serial measurement of hepatic enzyme levels does not prevent fulminant PTU-related hepatotoxicity.

95
00:13:09,758 --> 00:13:16,158
Second, an allergic rash may develop in 3 to 5 percent of women taking thionamide drugs .

96
00:13:16,158 --> 00:13:30,021
In addition, although approximately 20 percent of patients treated with PTU develop antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCA), only a small percentage of these subsequently develops serious vasculitis .

97
00:13:30,021 --> 00:13:36,233
Last, thionamides have the potential to cause fetal complications, but these are uncommon.

98
00:13:36,233 --> 00:13:41,246
In some cases, thionamides may even be therapeutic for the fetus.

99
00:13:41,246 --> 00:13:50,533
This is because TSH-receptor antibodies cross the placenta and can stimulate the fetal thyroid gland to cause thyrotoxicosis and goiter.

100
00:13:50,533 --> 00:13:53,858
The initial thionamide dose is empirical.

101
00:13:53,858 --> 00:14:07,321
For nonpregnant patients, the American Thyroid Association recommends that methimazole be used at an initial higher daily dose of 10 to 20 mg orally followed by a lower maintenance dose of 5 to 10 mg.

102
00:14:07,321 --> 00:14:16,858
If PTU is selected, a dose of 50 to 150 mg orally three times daily may be initiated depending on clinical severity.

103
00:14:16,858 --> 00:14:26,533
At Parkland Hospital, we usually initially give 300 or 450 mg of PTU daily in three divided doses for pregnant women.

104
00:14:26,533 --> 00:14:32,071
Occasionally, daily doses of 600 mg or higher are necessary.

105
00:14:32,071 --> 00:14:36,908
We routinely transition women to methimazole after the first trimester.

106
00:14:36,908 --> 00:14:44,171
If a switch is deemed appropriate, a dose ratio of 20:1—PTU to methimazole—is recommended .

107
00:14:44,171 --> 00:14:48,558
The goal is treatment with the lowest possible thionamide dose to

108
00:14:48,724 --> 00:14:56,274
maintain thyroid hormone levels slightly above or in the high normal range, while TSH levels remains suppressed.

109
00:14:56,224 --> 00:15:01,424
Serum free T4 concentrations are measured every 4 to 6 weeks.

110
00:15:01,424 --> 00:15:07,386
Subtotal thyroidectomy can be performed after thyrotoxicosis is medically controlled.

111
00:15:07,386 --> 00:15:21,011
This seldom is done during pregnancy but may be appropriate for the very few women who cannot adhere to medical treatment or in whom drug therapy proves toxic (Pearce, 2019; Stagnaro-Green, 2012a).

112
00:15:21,011 --> 00:15:24,786
Surgery is best accomplished in the second trimester.

113
00:15:24,786 --> 00:15:33,424
Potential drawbacks of thyroidectomy include inadvertent resection of parathyroid glands and injury to the recurrent laryngeal nerve.

114
00:15:33,424 --> 00:15:39,511
Thyroid ablation with therapeutic radioactive iodine is contraindicated in pregnancy .

115
00:15:39,511 --> 00:15:43,949
These doses may also cause fetal thyroid gland destruction.

116
00:15:43,949 --> 00:15:50,524
Thus, when radioactive iodine is given unintentionally, some clinicians recommend abortion.

117
00:15:50,524 --> 00:16:00,074
Any exposed fetus must be carefully evaluated, and the incidence of fetal hypothyroidism depends on gestational age and radioiodine dose .

118
00:16:00,074 --> 00:16:14,824
Importantly, several cases of inadvertent periconceptional exposure to radioactive iodine have been reported with no obvious ill effects for the offspring (Radacic-Aumiler, 2016; Sadakata, 2014).

119
00:16:14,824 --> 00:16:23,324
The International Commission on Radiological Protection recommends that women avoid pregnancy for 6 months after radioablative therapy .

120
00:16:23,324 --> 00:16:29,711
Moreover, during lactation, the breast also concentrates a substantial amount of iodine.

121
00:16:29,711 --> 00:16:38,036
This may pose a neonatal risk due to 131I-containing milk ingestion and maternal risk from significant breast irradiation.

122
00:16:38,036 --> 00:16:45,824
To limit the latter, a delay of 3 months after breastfeeding cessation will more reliably ensure complete breast involution.

123
00:16:45,824 --> 00:16:54,136
Pregnancy Outcome Women with thyrotoxicosis have pregnancy outcomes that largely depend on whether metabolic control is achieved.

124
00:16:54,136 --> 00:17:05,474
For example, excess thyroxine may cause miscarriage or preterm birth (Andersen, 2014; Moleti, 2019; Sheehan, 2015).

125
00:17:05,474 --> 00:17:16,049
In untreated women or in those who remain hyperthyroid despite therapy, incidences of preeclampsia, heart failure, and adverse perinatal outcomes are higher .

126
00:17:16,049 --> 00:17:26,461
One case-cohort study in the Danish National Birth Cohort found that maternal hyperthyroidism was also a risk for epilepsy and autism spectrum disorders in children .

127
00:17:26,461 --> 00:17:29,111
Table 61-2.

128
00:17:29,111 --> 00:17:37,811
Pregnancy Outcomes in Women with Overt Thyrotoxicosis Fetal and Neonatal Effects In most cases, the perinate is euthyroid.

129
00:17:37,811 --> 00:17:43,799
In some, however, hyper- or hypothyroidism can develop with or without a goiter .

130
00:17:43,799 --> 00:17:54,849
Clinical hyperthyroidism develops in up to 1 percent of neonates born to women with Graves disease (Barbesino, 2013; Fitzpatrick, 2010).

131
00:17:54,849 --> 00:18:01,924
If fetal thyroid disease is suspected, nomograms exist for sonographically measured thyroid volume .

132
00:18:01,924 --> 00:18:04,586
Figure 61-3.

133
00:18:04,586 --> 00:18:12,974
Term hypothyroid neonate delivered of a woman with a 3-year history of thyrotoxicosis that recurred at 26 weeks’ gestation.

134
00:18:12,974 --> 00:18:19,499
The mother was given methimazole, 30 mg orally daily, and was euthyroid at delivery.

135
00:18:19,499 --> 00:18:26,986
The fetus or neonate who was exposed to excessive maternal thyroxine may have any of several clinical presentations.

136
00:18:26,986 --> 00:18:34,349
First, goitrous thyrotoxicosis stems from placental transfer of thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins.

137
00:18:34,349 --> 00:18:43,474
Nonimmune hydrops and fetal demise can complicate fetal thyrotoxicosis (Nachum, 2003; Stulberg, 2000).

138
00:18:43,474 --> 00:18:56,861
The best predictor of perinatal thyrotoxicosis is the presence of thyroid-stimulating TSH-receptor antibodies in women with Graves disease (Moleti, 2019; Nathan, 2014).

139
00:18:56,861 --> 00:19:02,474
This is especially true if their levels are more than threefold higher than the upper normal limit .

140
00:19:02,474 --> 00:19:12,599
In one study of 72 pregnant women with Graves disease, none of the fetuses in 31 low-risk mothers had a goiter, and all were euthyroid at delivery .

141
00:19:12,599 --> 00:19:21,561
Low risk was defined as no requirement for antithyroid medications during the third trimester or an absence of antithyroid antibodies.

142
00:19:21,561 --> 00:19:36,911
Conversely, in a group of 41 women who either were taking antithyroid medication at delivery or had thyroid-receptor antibodies, 11 fetuses—27 percent—had sonographic evidence of a goiter at 32 weeks’ gestation.

143
00:19:36,911 --> 00:19:40,636
Seven of these 11 fetuses were determined to be

144
00:19:40,804 --> 00:19:45,179
hypothyroid, and the remaining fetuses were hyperthyroid.

145
00:19:45,129 --> 00:19:57,966
Because of these findings, the American Thyroid Association recommends routine evaluation of TSH-receptor antibodies (TRAb) in early pregnancy in women treated with antithyroid medication .

146
00:19:57,966 --> 00:20:07,016
In those who have elevated TRAb levels or who continue therapy, repeat testing is recommended between 18 and 22 weeks’ gestation.

147
00:20:07,016 --> 00:20:14,691
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020), however, does not recommend such testing.

148
00:20:14,691 --> 00:20:23,329
If the fetus is thyrotoxic, maternal thionamide drugs are increased even though maternal thyroid function may be within targeted range .

149
00:20:23,329 --> 00:20:35,016
If secondary maternal hypothyroidism develops, the fetus can be treated by a reduced maternal antithyroid medication dose and injections of intraamnionic thyroxine if necessary.

150
00:20:35,016 --> 00:20:46,829
Although usually shortlived, neonatal thyrotoxicosis may require a short course of antithyroid drug treatment (Levy-Shraga, 2014; Nathan, 2014).

151
00:20:46,829 --> 00:20:56,866
A second fetal presentation is goitrous hypothyroidism caused by fetal exposure to maternally administered thionamides (see Fig. 61-3).

152
00:20:56,866 --> 00:21:04,154
Despite theoretical neurological implications, reports of adverse fetal effects seem to have been exaggerated.

153
00:21:04,154 --> 00:21:15,241
For example, in at least 239 treated thyrotoxic women shown in Table 61-2, evidence of hypothyroidism was found in only four newborns.

154
00:21:15,241 --> 00:21:22,704
Furthermore, at least four long-term studies report no abnormal intellectual or physical development of these children .

155
00:21:22,704 --> 00:21:32,866
As long as the maternal free thyroxine level remains in the upper therapeutic to mildly thyrotoxic range, the likelihood of fetal hypothyroidism is remote .

156
00:21:32,866 --> 00:21:42,291
A third presentation, nongoitrous hypothyroidism, may develop from transplacental passage of maternal TSH-receptor blocking antibodies .

157
00:21:42,291 --> 00:21:54,566
And last, fetal thyrotoxicosis after maternal thyroid gland ablation, usually with 131I radioiodine, may result from transplacental thyroid-stimulating antibodies.

158
00:21:54,566 --> 00:22:05,704
In one report of early fetal exposure to radioiodine, neonatal thyroid studies indicated transient hyperthyroidism from maternal transfer of stimulating antibodies .

159
00:22:05,704 --> 00:22:11,254
Fetal Diagnosis Evaluation of fetal thyroid function remains controversial.

160
00:22:11,254 --> 00:22:27,891
Although the fetal thyroid volume can be measured sonographically in women taking thionamide drugs or in those with thyroid-stimulating antibodies, most investigators do not currently recommend this routinely (Cohen, 2003; Luton, 2005).

161
00:22:27,891 --> 00:22:37,604
Fetal hyper- or hypothyroidism may cause hydrops, growth restriction, goiter, or tachycardia, and fetal blood sampling may be appropriate .

162
00:22:37,604 --> 00:22:51,616
The American Thyroid Association guidelines for management of thyroid disease during pregnancy recommend that umbilical cord blood sampling only be considered when a fetal goiter is detected and the thyroid status of the fetus is unclear .

163
00:22:51,616 --> 00:22:57,466
Diagnosis and treatment are considered further in Chapter 19 (p. 370).

164
00:22:57,466 --> 00:23:03,654
Thyroid Storm and Thyrotoxic Heart Failure Both of these are acute and life-threatening in pregnancy.

165
00:23:03,654 --> 00:23:08,341
Thyroid storm is a hypermetabolic state and is rare in pregnancy.

166
00:23:08,341 --> 00:23:18,779
In contrast, pulmonary hypertension and heart failure from cardiomyopathy caused by the profound myocardial effects of thyroxine are common in pregnant women .

167
00:23:18,779 --> 00:23:27,279
As shown in Table 61-2, heart failure developed in 8 percent of 90 women with uncontrolled thyrotoxicosis.

168
00:23:27,279 --> 00:23:40,754
In a registry of almost 70,000 pregnancies from one province in China, six of 155 (4 percent) women diagnosed with thyrotoxicosis were found to have hyperthyroid heart disease .

169
00:23:40,754 --> 00:23:52,204
The pregnant woman with thyrotoxicosis has minimal cardiac reserve, and decompensation is usually precipitated by preeclampsia, anemia, sepsis, or a combination.

170
00:23:52,204 --> 00:24:03,029
Fortunately, thyroxine-induced cardiomyopathy and pulmonary hypertension are frequently reversible (Shan, 2019; Sheffield, 2004).

171
00:24:03,029 --> 00:24:13,354
Management Treatment is similar for thyroid storm and heart failure and should be carried out in an intensive care area that may include special-care units within labor and delivery .

172
00:24:13,354 --> 00:24:21,654
Shown in Figure 61-4 is our stepwise approach to medical management of thyroid storm or thyrotoxic heart failure.

173
00:24:21,654 --> 00:24:25,066
An hour or two after initial thionamide

174
00:24:25,228 --> 00:24:31,303
administration, iodide is given to inhibit thyroidal release of T3 and T4.

175
00:24:31,253 --> 00:24:41,928
It can be given intravenously (IV) as sodium iodide or orally as either saturated solution of potassium iodide (SSKI) or Lugol solution.

176
00:24:41,928 --> 00:24:50,478
With a history of iodine-induced anaphylaxis, lithium carbonate, 300 mg every 6 hours, is given instead.

177
00:24:50,478 --> 00:25:01,028
Most authorities recommend dexamethasone, 2 mg IV every 6 hours for four doses, to further block peripheral conversion of T4 to T3.

178
00:25:01,028 --> 00:25:07,690
If a β-blocking drug is given to control tachycardia, its effect on heart failure must be considered.

179
00:25:07,690 --> 00:25:12,090
Propranolol, labetalol, and esmolol are options.

180
00:25:12,090 --> 00:25:19,890
Coexisting severe preeclampsia, infection, or anemia are aggressively managed before delivery is considered.

181
00:25:19,890 --> 00:25:22,565
Figure 61-4.

182
00:25:22,565 --> 00:25:27,853
Parkland Hospital management for thyroid storm or thyrotoxic heart failure.

183
00:25:27,853 --> 00:25:38,490
gtt = drops; IV = intravenous; NGT = nasogastric tube; PO = orally; PTU = propylthiouracil.

184
00:25:38,490 --> 00:25:47,703
Hyperemesis Gravidarum Transient biochemical features of hyperthyroidism may be observed in 2 to 15 percent of women in early pregnancy .

185
00:25:47,703 --> 00:25:55,153
Many women with hyperemesis gravidarum have abnormally high serum T4 levels and low TSH levels .

186
00:25:55,153 --> 00:25:59,853
This results from TSH-receptor stimulation from hCG.

187
00:25:59,853 --> 00:26:05,565
This transient condition is also termed gestational transient thyrotoxicosis.

188
00:26:05,565 --> 00:26:10,903
Even if associated with hyperemesis, antithyroid drugs are not warranted .

189
00:26:10,903 --> 00:26:22,528
The hCG levels do not correlate with T4 and TSH values, which trend to normal by midpregnancy (Nathan, 2014; Yoshihara, 2015).

190
00:26:22,528 --> 00:26:32,203
Gestational Trophoblastic Disease The prevalence of T4 level elevation in women with a molar pregnancy ranges between 25 and 65 percent .

191
00:26:32,203 --> 00:26:39,078
As discussed, abnormally high hCG levels lead to overstimulation of the TSH receptor.

192
00:26:39,078 --> 00:26:46,153
Because these tumors are now usually diagnosed early, clinically apparent hyperthyroidism is less common.

193
00:26:46,153 --> 00:26:55,803
With molar evacuation, serum free T4 levels usually drop to normal levels rapidly in parallel with declining hCG concentrations.

194
00:26:55,803 --> 00:27:08,515
Subclinical Hyperthyroidism Third-generation TSH assays with an analytical sensitivity of 0.002 mU/mL permit identification of subclinical thyroid disorders.

195
00:27:08,515 --> 00:27:17,690
These biochemically defined extremes usually represent normal biological variations but may herald the earliest stages of thyroid dysfunction.

196
00:27:17,690 --> 00:27:27,178
Subclinical hyperthyroidism is characterized by an abnormally low serum TSH concentration in concert with normal T4 hormone levels.

197
00:27:27,178 --> 00:27:39,328
Long-term effects of persistent subclinical thyrotoxicosis include osteoporosis, cardiovascular morbidity, and progression to overt thyrotoxicosis or thyroid failure.

198
00:27:39,328 --> 00:27:48,603
In one recent metaanalysis of 20 studies, the prevalence of subclinical hyperthyroidism was 1.77 percent in pregnant women .

199
00:27:48,603 --> 00:27:55,465
Importantly, subclinical hyperthyroidism has not been associated with adverse pregnancy outcomes .

200
00:27:55,465 --> 00:28:14,940
In separate retrospective analyses of almost 25,000 women who underwent thyroid screening throughout pregnancy, investigators also found no relationship between subclinical hyperthyroidism and preeclampsia or gestational diabetes (Wilson, 2012; Tudela, 2012).

201
00:28:14,940 --> 00:28:22,390
Treatment of subclinical hyperthyroidism is unwarranted in pregnancy because antithyroid drugs may affect the fetus.

202
00:28:22,390 --> 00:28:30,565
These women may benefit from periodic surveillance, and approximately half eventually have normal TSH concentrations.

203
00:28:30,565 --> 00:28:33,178
# Hypothyroidism.

204
00:28:33,178 --> 00:28:44,978
Overt or symptomatic hypothyroidism complicates between 0.2 and 1.2 percent of pregnancies (Andersen, 2016; Casey, 2017).

205
00:28:44,978 --> 00:28:53,953
Its insidious, nonspecific clinical findings include fatigue, constipation, cold intolerance, muscle cramps, and weight gain.

206
00:28:53,953 --> 00:29:05,890
A pathologically enlarged thyroid gland depends on the etiology of hypothyroidism and is more likely in women in areas of endemic iodine deficiency or those with Hashimoto thyroiditis.

207
00:29:05,890 --> 00:29:13,703
Other findings are edema, dry skin, hair loss, and a prolonged relaxation phase of deep tendon reflexes.

208
00:29:13,703 --> 00:29:22,303
Clinical or overt hypothyroidism is confirmed by an abnormally high serum TSH level and an abnormally low T4 level.

209
00:29:22,303 --> 00:29:31,803
Subclinical hypothyroidism, discussed later, is defined by an elevated serum TSH level but normal serum T4 concentration .

210
00:29:31,803 --> 00:29:36,290
Asymptomatic individuals with high levels of anti-TPO or

211
00:29:36,460 --> 00:29:44,385
antithyroglobulin antibodies often are included in the spectrum of subclinical thyroid disease and will also be discussed.

212
00:29:44,335 --> 00:29:58,485
Overt Hypothyroidism and Pregnancy The most common cause of hypothyroidism in pregnancy is Hashimoto thyroiditis, characterized by glandular destruction from autoantibodies, particularly anti-TPO antibodies.

213
00:29:58,485 --> 00:30:01,997
Another cause is postablative Graves disease.

214
00:30:01,997 --> 00:30:11,422
Clinical identification of hypothyroidism is especially difficult during pregnancy because many of the signs or symptoms are also common pregnancy itself.

215
00:30:11,422 --> 00:30:21,585
Thyroid analyte testing should be performed on symptomatic women, those with a personal or family history of thyroid disease, or those with type 1 diabetes .

216
00:30:21,585 --> 00:30:30,810
Severe hypothyroidism during pregnancy is uncommon, probably because it is often associated with infertility and higher miscarriage rates .

217
00:30:30,810 --> 00:30:47,960
Treatment The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020) recommends oral replacement therapy for overt hypothyroidism beginning with levothyroxine in doses of 1 to 2 μg/kg/d or approximately 100 μg daily.

218
00:30:47,960 --> 00:30:54,760
Women who are athyreotic after thyroidectomy or radioiodine therapy may require higher doses.

219
00:30:54,760 --> 00:31:02,985
Surveillance is with TSH levels measured at 4-week intervals in the first half of pregnancy and at least once in the third trimester.

220
00:31:02,985 --> 00:31:12,597
The levothyroxine dose is adjusted by 25- to 50-μg increments until TSH values approximate 2.5 mU/L.

221
00:31:12,597 --> 00:31:23,272
Pregnancy is associated with an increased dose requirement in approximately a third of supplemented women (Abalovich, 2010; Alexander, 2004).

222
00:31:23,272 --> 00:31:29,110
The greater demand in pregnancy is believed to be related to augmented estrogen production .

223
00:31:29,110 --> 00:31:33,647
Higher dose requirements begin as early as 5 weeks’ gestation.

224
00:31:33,647 --> 00:31:51,372
Significant hypothyroidism may develop early in women without thyroid reserve, such as those with a previous thyroidectomy, those with prior radioiodine ablation, or those undergoing assisted reproductive techniques (Alexander, 2004; Loh, 2009).

225
00:31:51,372 --> 00:32:00,272
Theoretically, an anticipatory 25-percent increase in the levothyroxine dose at pregnancy confirmation will reduce this likelihood.

226
00:32:00,272 --> 00:32:10,047
However, one randomized trial compared empiric increases in dose against dose adjustments based on TSH levels and found both equally effective .

227
00:32:10,047 --> 00:32:14,910
Those in the empiric-dosing group had a higher risk of TSH suppression.

228
00:32:14,910 --> 00:32:24,897
At Parkland Hospital, all women with hypothyroidism undergo TSH testing at initiation of prenatal care and subsequent indicated dose adjustments.

229
00:32:24,897 --> 00:32:29,197
TSH determinations are repeated in each trimester.

230
00:32:29,197 --> 00:32:40,885
Pregnancy Outcome with Overt Hypothyroidism Observational studies, although limited, indicate that excessive adverse perinatal outcomes are associated with overt T4 deficiency .

231
00:32:40,885 --> 00:32:43,872
Preterm birth rates also are higher .

232
00:32:43,872 --> 00:32:56,285
With appropriate replacement therapy, however, rates of adverse effects are not increased in most reports (Bryant, 2015; Matalon, 2006; Tan, 2006).

233
00:32:56,285 --> 00:33:03,710
In one dissenting study, risks for some pregnancy complications were greater even in women taking replacement therapy .

234
00:33:03,710 --> 00:33:12,210
Most experts agree that adequate hormone replacement during pregnancy minimizes the risk of adverse outcomes and most complications.

235
00:33:12,210 --> 00:33:14,872
Table 61-3.

236
00:33:14,872 --> 00:33:25,622
Pregnancy Complications in 440 Women with Hypothyroidism Fetal and Neonatal Effects Undoubtedly, maternal and fetal thyroid abnormalities are related.

237
00:33:25,622 --> 00:33:35,960
In both, thyroid function is dependent on adequate iodine intake, and its deficiency early in pregnancy can cause both maternal and fetal hypothyroidism.

238
00:33:35,960 --> 00:33:48,835
In one analysis of 4273 mother-child pairs, an association between maternal thyroid function during pregnancy and both neonatal and childhood thyroid function was confirmed .

239
00:33:48,835 --> 00:33:56,847
As discussed, maternal TSH-receptorblocking antibodies can cross the placenta and cause fetal thyroid dysfunction.

240
00:33:56,847 --> 00:34:05,435
Rovelli and colleagues (2010) evaluated 129 neonates born to women with autoimmune thyroiditis.

241
00:34:05,435 --> 00:34:15,847
They found that 28 percent had an elevated TSH level on the third or fourth day of life, and 47 percent of these had TPO antibodies on day 15.

242
00:34:15,847 --> 00:34:20,572
Still, autoantibodies were undetectable at age 6 months.

243
00:34:20,572 --> 00:34:28,847
In their analysis, Korevaar and coworkers (2016a) concluded that maternal TPO antibodies have no

244
00:34:28,996 --> 00:34:33,271
clinically relevant effects on the thyroid function of offspring.

245
00:34:33,221 --> 00:34:43,183
Indeed, prevalence of fetal hypothyroidism in women with Hashimoto thyroiditis is estimated to be only 1 case in 180,000 newborns .

246
00:34:43,183 --> 00:34:46,558
# Subclinical Hypothyroidism.

247
00:34:46,558 --> 00:34:57,783
Although common in women, the incidence of subclinical hypothyroidism varies depending on age, race, dietary iodine intake, and serum TSH thresholds used .

248
00:34:57,783 --> 00:35:13,183
In two large studies totaling more than 25,000 pregnant women screened in the first half of pregnancy, subclinical hypothyroidism was identified in 2.3 percent (Casey, 2005; Cleary-Goldman, 2008).

249
00:35:13,183 --> 00:35:24,958
The rate of progression to overt thyroid failure is affected by TSH level, age, other disorders such as diabetes, and presence and concentration of antithyroid antibodies.

250
00:35:24,958 --> 00:35:38,021
Diez and Iglesias (2004) prospectively followed 93 nonpregnant women with subclinical hypothyroidism for 5 years and reported that in a third, TSH values became normal.

251
00:35:38,021 --> 00:35:48,446
In the other two thirds, those women whose TSH levels were 10 to 15 mU/L developed overt disease at a rate of 19 cases per 100 patient-years.

252
00:35:48,446 --> 00:35:57,596
Those women whose TSH levels were <10 mU/L developed overt hypothyroidism at a rate of 2 cases per 100 patient-years.

253
00:35:57,596 --> 00:36:18,296
Regarding screening for subclinical hypothyroidism in nonpregnant individuals, the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force also reports that nearly all patients who develop overt hypothyroidism within 5 years have an initial TSH level >10 mU/L (Helfand, 2004; Karmisholt, 2008).

254
00:36:18,296 --> 00:36:34,946
From one metaanalysis of data from nonpregnant adults with subclinical hypothyroidism, thyroid hormone therapy was not associated with improved general quality of life, thyroid-related symptoms, depression, blood pressure, or other cardiovascular outcomes .

255
00:36:34,946 --> 00:36:44,283
In a 20-year follow-up study of 5805 women who were screened in early pregnancy, only 3 percent developed thyroid disease.

256
00:36:44,283 --> 00:37:01,471
Of the 224 women identified with subclinical hypothyroidism during pregnancy, 17 percent developed thyroid disease in the next 20 years, and most of these had either TPO or thyroglobulin antibodies during pregnancy (Männistö, 2010).

257
00:37:01,471 --> 00:37:11,158
Thus, the likelihood of progression to overt hypothyroidism during pregnancy in otherwise healthy women with subclinical hypothyroidism seems remote.

258
00:37:11,158 --> 00:37:28,171
Subclinical Hypothyroidism and Pregnancy Interest in this subject was heightened 20 years ago by two studies reporting that undiagnosed maternal thyroid hypofunction may impair fetal neuropsychological development (Haddow, 1999; Pop, 1999).

259
00:37:28,171 --> 00:37:38,571
Notably, in the Haddow study, many women had both elevated TSH and abnormally low serum free T4 levels and thus had overt hypothyroidism.

260
00:37:38,571 --> 00:37:54,121
To evaluate any adverse pregnancy effects, Casey and colleagues (2005) identified subclinical hypothyroidism in 2.3 percent of 17,298 women screened at Parkland Hospital before midpregnancy.

261
00:37:54,121 --> 00:38:05,283
These women had small but significantly higher incidences of preterm birth, placental abruption, and neonates admitted to the intensive care nursery compared with euthyroid women.

262
00:38:05,283 --> 00:38:15,433
In another study of 10,990 similar women, however, ClearyGoldman and associates (2008) did not find such associations.

263
00:38:15,433 --> 00:38:25,983
Other studies subsequently confirmed a link between subclinical thyroid function and adverse outcomes (Chen, 2017; Maraka, 2016).

264
00:38:25,983 --> 00:38:35,421
One included 24,883 women screened throughout pregnancy and showed an almost twofold greater risk of severe preeclampsia .

265
00:38:35,421 --> 00:38:44,721
In an analysis of the latter cohort, a consistent relationship was shown between rising TSH levels and the risk for gestational diabetes .

266
00:38:44,721 --> 00:38:52,321
Last, Nelson and associates (2014) found an elevated risk for diabetes and stillbirth.

267
00:38:52,321 --> 00:39:02,296
Lazarus and coworkers (2012) reported the findings of the international multicenter Controlled Antenatal Thyroid Screening (CATS) study.

268
00:39:02,296 --> 00:39:11,933
They evaluated prenatal thyroid screening with randomized treatment of both subclinical hypothyroidism and isolated maternal hypothyroxinemia.

269
00:39:11,933 --> 00:39:18,183
They found that offspring IQ scores at age 3 years were not superior in the treated cohort.

270
00:39:18,183 --> 00:39:24,946
Later evaluation of these offspring through age 9 confirmed that maternal treatment offered no benefit .

271
00:39:24,946 --> 00:39:28,358
Last, the Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units

272
00:39:28,516 --> 00:39:39,103
MFMU) Network screened more than 97,000 pregnant women for thyroid disorders and reported that 3.3 percent had subclinical hypothyroidism.

273
00:39:39,053 --> 00:39:45,866
These 677 women were randomly assigned to thyroxine replacement therapy or placebo.

274
00:39:45,866 --> 00:39:59,016
As reported by Casey and colleagues (2017) and shown in Table 61-4, adverse pregnancy outcomes or cognitive development in the offspring at 5 years did not differ between groups.

275
00:39:59,016 --> 00:40:06,966
Annual developmental testing scores and behavioral and attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder results also did not differ.

276
00:40:06,966 --> 00:40:09,628
Table 61-4.

277
00:40:09,628 --> 00:40:26,653
Maternal-Fetal Medicine Unites Network: Pregnancy Outcomes According to Diagnosis and Treatment of Thyroid Disordersa Because of earlier study findings, some professional organizations began to recommend routine prenatal screening and treatment of subclinical hypothyroidism.

278
00:40:26,653 --> 00:40:51,166
However, after the CATS and MFMU Network studies, more recent clinical practice guidelines from the Endocrine Society, the American Thyroid Association, and the American Association of Clinical Endocrinologists now uniformly recommended screening only those at greater risk during pregnancy (Alexander, 2017; De Groot, 2012; Garber, 2012).

279
00:40:51,166 --> 00:40:58,753
This has been and still is the recommendation of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020).

280
00:40:58,753 --> 00:41:10,166
Isolated Maternal Hypothyroxinemia Women with low serum free T4 values but a normal-range TSH level are considered to have isolated maternal hypothyroxinemia.

281
00:41:10,166 --> 00:41:19,491
Its incidence in two large trials was 1.3 to 2.1 percent (Casey, 2007; Cleary-Goldman, 2008).

282
00:41:19,491 --> 00:41:28,778
As shown in Figure 61-2, unlike in subclinical hypothyroidism, these women have a low prevalence of antithyroid antibodies.

283
00:41:28,778 --> 00:41:42,828
Initial studies reported that offspring of women with isolated hypothyroxinemia had neurodevelopmental difficulties (Levie, 2018; Li, 2010; Pop, 1999, 2003).

284
00:41:42,828 --> 00:41:52,966
Casey and colleagues (2007), however, found no higher risks in these women for other adverse perinatal outcomes compared with those of euthyroid women.

285
00:41:52,966 --> 00:42:06,678
In contrast, a recent individual patient data systematic analysis including 47,045 pregnant women identified a higher risk of preterm birth associated with isolated hypothyroxinemia .

286
00:42:06,678 --> 00:42:24,178
The aforementioned CATS study, including follow-up of offspring through age 9 years, did not find improved neurodevelopmental outcomes in women with isolated hypothyroxinemia who were then treated with thyroxine (Hales, 2018; Lazarus, 2012).

287
00:42:24,178 --> 00:42:34,191
The randomized trial conducted by the MFMU Network also demonstrated no improvement in neurocognitive development with thyroxine treatment during pregnancy .

288
00:42:34,191 --> 00:42:44,166
This large randomized study also found no higher rates of adverse pregnancy outcomes between groups and found that early thyroxine treatment offered no benefits .

289
00:42:44,166 --> 00:42:52,528
The American Thyroid Association currently recommends against routine treatment of isolated hypothyroxinemia in pregnancy .

290
00:42:52,528 --> 00:42:56,378
# Euthyroid Autoimmune Thyroid Disease.

291
00:42:56,378 --> 00:43:04,316
Autoantibodies to TPO and thyroglobulin have been identified in 2 to 17 percent of pregnant women worldwide .

292
00:43:04,316 --> 00:43:09,453
Most who test positive for such antibodies, however, are euthyroid.

293
00:43:09,453 --> 00:43:20,391
That said, such women carry a two- to fivefold increased risk for early pregnancy loss (StagnaroGreen, 2004; Thangaratinam, 2011).

294
00:43:20,391 --> 00:43:25,753
The presence of thyroid antibodies has also been associated with preterm birth .

295
00:43:25,753 --> 00:43:37,403
In a randomized treatment trial of 115 euthyroid women with TPO antibodies, levothyroxine astoundingly reduced the preterm birth rate from 22 to 7 percent .

296
00:43:37,403 --> 00:43:47,541
In a follow-up trial restricted to women with a TSH <2.5 mU/L, however, levothyroxine treatment had no effect on this risk .

297
00:43:47,541 --> 00:44:05,491
In another study, Abbassi-Ghanavati and associates (2010) evaluated pregnancy outcomes in more than 1000 untreated women with TPO antibodies and did not find a higher risk for preterm birth compared with the risk in 16,000 euthyroid women without antibodies.

298
00:44:05,491 --> 00:44:12,228
These investigators, however, did find a threefold greater risk of placental abruption in these women.

299
00:44:12,228 --> 00:44:25,916
More recently, the Consortium on Thyroid and Pregnancy-Study Group on Preterm Birth (2019) estimated a marginal 1.3-fold increased risk for preterm birth in their metaanalysis of women with

300
00:44:26,068 --> 00:44:28,368
TPO antibodies.

301
00:44:28,318 --> 00:44:43,968
As with nonpregnant subjects with TPO antibodies, pregnant women are also at increased risk in later life for progression of thyroid disease and postpartum thyroiditis (Jameson, 2015; Stagnaro-Green, 2012a).

302
00:44:43,968 --> 00:45:00,318
Currently, universal screening for the thyroid autoantibodies is not recommended by any professional organization (Alexander, 2017; American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; De Groot, 2012).

303
00:45:00,318 --> 00:45:03,105
# Iodine Deficiency.

304
00:45:03,105 --> 00:45:17,043
Decreasing iodide fortification of table salt and bread products in the United States during the past 25 years has led to occasional iodide deficiency (Caldwell, 2005; Hollowell, 1998).

305
00:45:17,043 --> 00:45:27,505
Importantly, the most recent National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey indicated that, overall, the United States population remains iodine sufficient .

306
00:45:27,505 --> 00:45:36,380
However, experts agree that iodine nutrition in vulnerable populations, such as pregnant women, requires continued monitoring.

307
00:45:36,380 --> 00:45:48,005
In 2014, the Office of Dietary Supplements of the National Institutes of Health sponsored three workshops to identify information gaps and develop an iodine research agenda.

308
00:45:48,005 --> 00:45:57,655
Participants emphasized the gradual decline in median urinary iodine levels in pregnant women and the serious potential effects on developing fetuses .

309
00:45:57,655 --> 00:46:08,055
Dietary iodine requirements are higher during pregnancy due to augmented thyroid hormone production, increased renal losses, and fetal iodine requirements.

310
00:46:08,055 --> 00:46:17,593
Adequate iodine is requisite for fetal neurological development beginning soon after conception, and abnormalities vary by the degree of deficiency.

311
00:46:17,593 --> 00:46:31,243
For example, Levie and colleagues (2019) demonstrated a positive correlation between maternal urinary iodine/creatinine ratio in the first trimester and offspring nonverbal IQ scores.

312
00:46:31,243 --> 00:46:44,068
UNICEF (2018) estimates that nearly 19 million babies born globally every year are at risk of permanent yet preventable brain damage or of reduced cognitive function from iodine deficiency.

313
00:46:44,068 --> 00:46:51,493
Although it is doubtful that mild deficiency causes intellectual impairment, supplementation does prevent fetal goiter .

314
00:46:51,493 --> 00:47:02,793
In one randomized trial with more than 800 women who had mild deficiency, daily iodine supplementation had no effect on child neurodevelopment at age 5 to 6 years .

315
00:47:02,793 --> 00:47:11,505
Supplementation studies conducted in regions with moderate deficiency have reported inconsistent findings with regard to psychomotor development .

316
00:47:11,505 --> 00:47:21,318
Berbel and associates (2009) began daily supplementation in more than 300 pregnant women with moderate deficiency at three time periods.

317
00:47:21,318 --> 00:47:31,293
They found improved neurobehavioral development scores in offspring of women supplemented with 200 μg of potassium iodide very early in pregnancy.

318
00:47:31,293 --> 00:47:44,305
Similarly, Velasco and coworkers (2009) found improved Bayley Psychomotor Development scores in offspring of women supplemented with 300 μg of iodine daily in the first trimester.

319
00:47:44,305 --> 00:47:56,730
In contrast, Murcia and colleagues (2011) identified lower psychomotor scores in 1year-old infants whose mothers reported daily supplementation of more than 150 μg.

320
00:47:56,730 --> 00:48:08,005
Severe deficiency is frequently associated with damage typically encountered with endemic cretinism, and benefits of universal salt iodization or supplementation have been confirmed .

321
00:48:08,005 --> 00:48:20,668
Regarding daily iodine intake, the Institute of Medicine (2001) recommends 220 μg/d during pregnancy and 290 μg/d during lactation .

322
00:48:20,668 --> 00:48:41,230
The American Thyroid Association recommends an average iodine intake of 250 μg/d in all pregnant women and has recommended that 150 μg of iodine be added to prenatal vitamins to achieve this average daily oral intake (Alexander, 2017; Kerver, 2021).

323
00:48:41,230 --> 00:48:51,155
In an older study, Leung and coworkers (2011) reported that only half of the prenatal multivitamins in the United States contained iodine.

324
00:48:51,155 --> 00:49:01,930
Without evidence of benefit, however, it is hard to justify the cost of iodine supplementation of large numbers of pregnant women in areas with mild iodine deficiency .

325
00:49:01,930 --> 00:49:16,855
Importantly, experts caution against oversupplementation since excessive iodine exposure can lead to subclinical thyroid dysfunction and is associated with increased prevalence of thyroid autoimmunity (Pearce 2016; Shi,

326
00:49:17,020 --> 00:49:27,607
2015). The American Thyroid Association advises against exceeding twice the daily recommended intake of iodine, which is 500 μg/d .

327
00:49:27,557 --> 00:49:30,807
# Congenital Hypothyroidism.

328
00:49:30,807 --> 00:49:39,820
Universal newborn screening for neonatal hypothyroidism was introduced in 1974 and is now required by law in all states .

329
00:49:39,820 --> 00:49:43,995
This develops in approximately 1 in 2000 newborns.

330
00:49:43,995 --> 00:49:54,457
The reported incidence has increased during the past few decades and is thought to stem from greater preterm neonate survival rates and methodological shifts in newborn screening .

331
00:49:54,457 --> 00:50:04,107
Variation in TSH percentiles according to gestational age at delivery have led some to propose age-adjusted cutoffs to avoid overdiagnosis .

332
00:50:04,107 --> 00:50:12,095
Developmental disorders of the thyroid gland such as agenesis and hypoplasia account for 80 to 90 percent of cases.

333
00:50:12,095 --> 00:50:26,295
The remainder may be caused by hereditary defects in thyroid hormone production, but it is estimated that up to 15 percent may have a transient form of the disease (McGrath, 2018; Oron, 2018).

334
00:50:26,295 --> 00:50:33,895
Early and aggressive thyroxine replacement is critical for affected newborns (van Trotsenburg, 2021).

335
00:50:33,895 --> 00:50:46,895
In one study of 76 children with congenital hypothyroidism, IQ scores at age 18 years did not differ from those in 40 siblings, provided the initial thyroxine dose was sufficiently high .

336
00:50:46,895 --> 00:50:49,982
# Postpartum Thyroiditis.

337
00:50:49,982 --> 00:51:02,732
Transient autoimmune thyroiditis is consistently found in approximately 5 to 10 percent of women during the first year after childbirth (Nathan, 2014; Nguyen, 2019).

338
00:51:02,732 --> 00:51:11,082
With an onset within 12 months, postpartum thyroid dysfunction includes hyperthyroidism, hypothyroidism, or both.

339
00:51:11,082 --> 00:51:19,670
The propensity for thyroiditis antedates pregnancy and is directly related to increasing serum levels of thyroid autoantibodies.

340
00:51:19,670 --> 00:51:27,270
Up to 50 percent of women who have thyroid antibodies in the first trimester will develop postpartum thyroiditis .

341
00:51:27,270 --> 00:51:34,620
As mentioned previously, routine thyroid autoantibody testing during pregnancy is currently not recommended .

342
00:51:34,620 --> 00:51:44,195
In clinical practice, postpartum thyroiditis is diagnosed infrequently because it typically develops months after delivery and causes vague symptoms .

343
00:51:44,195 --> 00:51:51,082
The presentation varies, and classically two clinical phases that may develop in succession are recognized.

344
00:51:51,082 --> 00:51:59,182
The first and earliest is destruction-induced thyrotoxicosis with symptoms from excessive release of hormone from glandular disruption.

345
00:51:59,182 --> 00:52:03,695
The onset is abrupt, and a small, painless goiter is common.

346
00:52:03,695 --> 00:52:12,070
From many possible symptoms, only fatigue and palpitations are more frequent in thyrotoxic women compared with normal controls.

347
00:52:12,070 --> 00:52:16,307
This thyrotoxic phase usually lasts only a few months.

348
00:52:16,307 --> 00:52:22,432
Thionamides are ineffective, and if symptoms are severe, a β-blocking agent may be given.

349
00:52:22,432 --> 00:52:29,682
The second and usually later phase between 4 and 8 months postpartum is hypothyroidism from thyroiditis.

350
00:52:29,682 --> 00:52:35,732
Thyromegaly and other symptoms are common and more prominent than during the thyrotoxic phase.

351
00:52:35,732 --> 00:52:44,157
Levothyroxine replacement at doses of 25 to 75 μg/d is typically given for 6 to 12 months.

352
00:52:44,157 --> 00:52:55,932
Stagnaro-Green and associates (2011) reported postpartum surveillance results in 4562 Italian gravidas who had been screened for thyroid disease in pregnancy.

353
00:52:55,932 --> 00:53:02,370
Serum TSH and anti-TPO antibody levels were measured again at 6 and 12 months.

354
00:53:02,370 --> 00:53:13,482
Overall, 169 women (3.9 percent) developed postpartum thyroiditis, and two thirds of these were identified to have hypothyroidism only.

355
00:53:13,482 --> 00:53:17,245
The other third were diagnosed with hyperthyroidism.

356
00:53:17,245 --> 00:53:22,620
Only 14 percent of all women demonstrated the classic biphasic progression.

357
00:53:22,620 --> 00:53:29,882
These findings are consistent with data compiled from 20 other studies between 1982 and 2008 .

358
00:53:29,882 --> 00:53:43,257
Importantly, women who experience either type of postpartum thyroiditis have a 20- to 30-percent risk of eventually developing permanent hypothyroidism, and the annual progression rate is 3.6 percent .

359
00:53:43,257 --> 00:53:53,695
Women at greater risk for developing hypothyroidism are those with higher titers of thyroid antibodies and higher TSH levels during the initial hypothyroid phase.

360
00:53:53,695 --> 00:54:04,645
Others may develop subclinical disease, but half of those with thyroiditis who are positive for TPO antibodies develop permanent hypothyroidism by 6 to 7 years .

361
00:54:04,645 --> 00:54:07,007
An association between

362
00:54:07,156 --> 00:54:13,381
postpartum thyroiditis and postpartum depression has been proposed but remains unresolved .

363
00:54:13,331 --> 00:54:18,356
Similarly unsettled is the link between depression and thyroid antibodies.

364
00:54:18,356 --> 00:54:27,068
Kuijpens and associates (2001) reported that TPO antibodies were a marker for postpartum depression in euthyroid women.

365
00:54:27,068 --> 00:54:40,906
In a randomized trial, however, Harris and coworkers (2002) reported no difference in postpartum depression in 342 women with TPO antibodies who were given either levothyroxine or placebo.

366
00:54:40,906 --> 00:54:53,193
Somewhat related, in the MFMU Network randomized trial, women with subclinical hypothyroidism allocated to thyroxine treatment during pregnancy did not experience improvement in their depression .

367
00:54:53,193 --> 00:54:56,218
# Nodular Thyroid Disease.

368
00:54:56,218 --> 00:55:01,906
Palpable thyroid nodules can be found in up to 5 percent of reproductiveaged women .

369
00:55:01,906 --> 00:55:08,581
Management of a palpable thyroid nodule during pregnancy depends on gestational age and mass size.

370
00:55:08,581 --> 00:55:15,793
Small nodules detected by sensitive sonographic methods are more common during pregnancy in some populations.

371
00:55:15,793 --> 00:55:26,518
Kung and associates (2002) used highresolution sonography and found that 15 percent of Chinese women had nodules larger than 2 mm in diameter.

372
00:55:26,518 --> 00:55:29,906
Almost half of cases had multiple masses.

373
00:55:29,906 --> 00:55:35,793
The nodules usually enlarged modestly across pregnancy and did not regress postpartum.

374
00:55:35,793 --> 00:55:44,368
Biopsy of those measuring >5 mm3 that persisted at 3 months usually showed nodular hyperplasia, and none was malignant.

375
00:55:44,368 --> 00:55:49,968
In most studies, 90 to 95 percent of solitary nodules are benign .

376
00:55:49,968 --> 00:55:56,431
Evaluation of thyroid nodules during pregnancy should be similar to that for nonpregnant patients.

377
00:55:56,431 --> 00:56:04,968
As discussed in Chapter 49 (p. 875), radioiodine scanning in pregnancy is usually not recommended .

378
00:56:04,968 --> 00:56:13,968
Sonographic examination reliably detects nodules >5 mm, and their solid or cystic structure also is determined.

379
00:56:13,968 --> 00:56:27,068
According to the American Association of Clinical Endocrinologists, sonographic characteristics associated with malignancy include hypoechogenic pattern, irregular margins, and microcalcifications .

380
00:56:27,068 --> 00:56:38,781
Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) under ultrasound guidance is an excellent assessment method because cytological diagnostic criteria are not substantially influenced by pregnancy .

381
00:56:38,781 --> 00:56:45,106
If the FNA biopsy shows a follicular lesion, surgery may be deferred until postpartum.

382
00:56:45,106 --> 00:56:50,056
Evaluation of thyroid cancer involves a multidisciplinary approach .

383
00:56:50,056 --> 00:56:54,643
Most thyroid carcinomas are well differentiated and indolent.

384
00:56:54,643 --> 00:57:03,881
Messuti and coworkers (2014) provided evidence that persistence or recurrence of these tumors may be more common in pregnant women.

385
00:57:03,881 --> 00:57:12,293
For thyroid malignancy diagnosed in the first or second trimester, thyroidectomy may be performed before the third trimester.

386
00:57:12,293 --> 00:57:22,156
In women without evidence of an aggressive thyroid cancer or in those diagnosed in the third trimester, surgical treatment can be deferred until the early puerperium .

387
00:57:22,156 --> 00:57:24,381
Parathyroid Disease.

388
00:57:24,381 --> 00:57:30,918
Parathyroid hormone (PTH) maintains extracellular fluid calcium concentration.

389
00:57:30,918 --> 00:57:39,318
This 84-amino-acid hormone acts directly on bone and kidney and indirectly on small intestine to increase serum calcium .

390
00:57:39,318 --> 00:57:46,568
PTH secretion is regulated by serum ionized calcium concentration through a negative feedback system.

391
00:57:46,568 --> 00:57:53,118
Calcitonin is a potent parathyroid hormone that acts as a physiological PTH antagonist.

392
00:57:53,118 --> 00:58:05,518
The relationships between these hormones, calcium metabolism, and PTH-related protein (PTH-rP) produced by fetal tissue are discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 73).

393
00:58:05,518 --> 00:58:14,343
Of fetal demands, calcium requirements reach 300 mg/d in late pregnancy and 30 g for the entire gestation.

394
00:58:14,343 --> 00:58:22,993
These needs and greater renal calcium loss from augmented maternal glomerular filtration substantively raise maternal calcium demands.

395
00:58:22,993 --> 00:58:31,293
Total serum calcium levels decline with serum albumin concentrations, but ionized calcium levels remain unchanged.

396
00:58:31,293 --> 00:58:42,518
Pregnancy is also associated with a twofold rise in serum concentrations of 1,25dihydroxyvitamin D, which increases gastrointestinal calcium absorption.

397
00:58:42,518 --> 00:58:51,631
The effectuating hormone is probably of placental and decidual origin since maternal PTH levels are low normal or decreased during pregnancy .

398
00:58:51,631 --> 00:58:57,231
Calcium is actively transported across the placenta and deposited in the developing

399
00:58:57,388 --> 00:58:59,663
fetal skeletal system.

400
00:58:59,613 --> 00:59:10,963
Fetal calcium homeostasis is maintained through a complex interplay between PTH-rP, PTH, and receptors in the placenta, fetal kidney, and fetal skeleton .

401
00:59:10,963 --> 00:59:13,913
# Hyperparathyroidism.

402
00:59:13,913 --> 00:59:20,188
Hypercalcemia is caused by hyperparathyroidism or cancer in 90 percent of cases .

403
00:59:20,188 --> 00:59:32,138
Because many automated laboratory systems include serum calcium measurement, hyperparathyroidism has changed from being a condition defined by symptoms to one that is discovered on routine screening .

404
00:59:32,138 --> 00:59:43,700
Hypercalcemia has a reported prevalence of 2 to 3 cases per 1000 women, but some estimate the rate to reach 14 cases per 1000 if asymptomatic women are included.

405
00:59:43,700 --> 00:59:51,950
Almost 80 percent are caused by a solitary adenoma, and another 15 percent by hyperfunctioning of all four glands.

406
00:59:51,950 --> 00:59:56,688
In the remainder, an associated malignancy is usually obvious.

407
00:59:56,688 --> 01:00:04,575
Of note, PTH produced by tumors is not identical to the natural hormone and may not be detected by routine assays.

408
01:00:04,575 --> 01:00:15,425
In most patients with hyperparathyroidism, the serum calcium level is elevated to within only 1 to 1.5 mg/dL above the upper normal limit.

409
01:00:15,425 --> 01:00:22,288
This may help to explain why only 20 percent of those who have abnormally elevated levels are symptomatic .

410
01:00:22,288 --> 01:00:28,638
In a fourth, however, symptoms become apparent when the serum calcium level continues to rise.

411
01:00:28,638 --> 01:00:36,188
Hypercalcemic crisis manifests as stupor, nausea, vomiting, weakness, fatigue, and dehydration.

412
01:00:36,188 --> 01:00:44,713
Women with symptomatic hyperparathyroidism should be surgically treated (Davis, 2020; Potts, 2015).

413
01:00:44,713 --> 01:01:00,825
Indications for parathyroidectomy include a serum calcium level 1.0 mg/dL above the upper normal range, a calculated creatinine clearance <60 mL/min, reduced bone density, or age >50 years .

414
01:01:00,825 --> 01:01:10,175
Those not meeting these criteria should undergo annual serum calcium and creatinine level measurement and bone density assessment every 1 to 2 years .

415
01:01:10,175 --> 01:01:22,313
Hyperparathyroidism in Pregnancy In their review, Schnatz and Thaxton (2005) found fewer than 200 reported cases of hyperparathyroidism complicating pregnancy.

416
01:01:22,313 --> 01:01:28,200
As in nonpregnant patients, parathyroid adenoma is the most common etiology.

417
01:01:28,200 --> 01:01:39,100
Ectopic parathyroid hormone production and rare cases of parathyroid carcinoma have been reported in pregnancy (Montoro, 2000; Saad, 2014).

418
01:01:39,100 --> 01:01:48,688
Symptoms of hyperparathyroidism during pregnancy include hyperemesis, generalized weakness, renal calculi, and psychiatric disorders.

419
01:01:48,688 --> 01:01:52,838
Occasionally, pancreatitis is the presenting disorder .

420
01:01:52,838 --> 01:02:01,650
Pregnancy theoretically improves hyperparathyroidism by significantly shunting calcium to the fetus and by augmented renal excretion.

421
01:02:01,650 --> 01:02:07,788
When these effects are withdrawn, however, postpartum hypercalcemic crisis is a danger.

422
01:02:07,788 --> 01:02:20,975
This life-threatening complication can be seen with serum calcium levels >14 mg/dL, and nausea, vomiting, tremors, dehydration, and mental status changes are symptoms .

423
01:02:20,975 --> 01:02:28,900
Early reports described excessive stillbirths and preterm deliveries in pregnancies complicated by hyperparathyroidism.

424
01:02:28,900 --> 01:02:36,225
More recent reports, however, describe lower rates of stillbirth, neonatal death, and neonatal tetany .

425
01:02:36,225 --> 01:02:42,438
Other fetal complications include miscarriage, fetalgrowth restriction, and low birthweight .

426
01:02:42,438 --> 01:02:46,825
One group reported a 30-percent incidence of preeclampsia .

427
01:02:46,825 --> 01:02:53,125
Management in Pregnancy Surgical removal of a symptomatic parathyroid adenoma is preferable.

428
01:02:53,125 --> 01:02:59,075
This should prevent fetal and neonatal morbidities and postpartum parathyroid crises .

429
01:02:59,075 --> 01:03:08,463
In one case series from an Australian referral hospital, 22 pregnancies were managed medically, and six women underwent parathyroidectomy.

430
01:03:08,463 --> 01:03:17,625
Preterm delivery was reported in 66 percent of pregnancies managed without surgery, whereas all surgically treated women delivered at term .

431
01:03:17,625 --> 01:03:28,200
Elective neck exploration during pregnancy is usually well tolerated, even in the third trimester (Hirsch, 2015; Stringer, 2017).

432
01:03:28,200 --> 01:03:37,400
In at least two cases, a mediastinal adenoma was removed at midpregnancy (Rooney, 1998; Saad, 2014).

433
01:03:37,400 --> 01:03:44,213
Medical management may however be appropriate in asymptomatic pregnant women with mild hypercalcemia .

434
01:03:44,213 --> 01:03:50,213
If so, patients are carefully monitored in the puerperium for hypercalcemic crisis.

435
01:03:50,213 --> 01:03:54,000
Initial medical management might include calcitonin

436
01:03:54,172 --> 01:04:03,584
to decrease skeletal calcium release, or oral phosphate, 1 to 1.5 g daily in divided doses, to bind excess calcium.

437
01:04:03,534 --> 01:04:13,597
For women with dangerously elevated serum calcium levels or those who are mentally obtunded with hypercalcemic crisis, emergency treatment is instituted.

438
01:04:13,597 --> 01:04:25,684
Diuresis with IV normal saline is begun so that urine flow exceeds 150 mL/hr. Furosemide is given in conventional doses to block tubular calcium reabsorption.

439
01:04:25,684 --> 01:04:30,697
Importantly, hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia should be prevented.

440
01:04:30,697 --> 01:04:35,797
Adjunctive therapy includes mithramycin, which inhibits bone resorption.

441
01:04:35,797 --> 01:04:41,847
Neonatal Effects Normally, cord blood calcium levels are higher than maternal levels .

442
01:04:41,847 --> 01:04:50,359
With maternal hyperparathyroidism, abnormally elevated maternal and thus fetal levels further suppress fetal parathyroid function.

443
01:04:50,359 --> 01:05:01,322
Because of this, newborn calcium levels rapidly drop after birth, and 15 to 25 percent of these neonates develop severe hypocalcemia with or without tetany .

444
01:05:01,322 --> 01:05:13,384
Neonatal hypoparathyroidism caused by maternal hyperparathyroidism is usually transient and is treated with calcium and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3 (calcitriol).

445
01:05:13,384 --> 01:05:21,897
The latter will not be effective in preterm infants, however, because the intestinal vitamin D receptor is insufficiently expressed .

446
01:05:21,897 --> 01:05:31,659
Neonatal tetany or seizures should stimulate an evaluation for maternal hyperparathyroidism (Beattie, 2000; Ip, 2003).

447
01:05:31,659 --> 01:05:34,584
# Hypoparathyroidism.

448
01:05:34,584 --> 01:05:42,672
The most common cause of hypocalcemia is hypoparathyroidism that usually follows parathyroid or thyroid surgery.

449
01:05:42,672 --> 01:05:49,647
It is estimated that almost 80 percent of cases are attributable to complications of anterior neck surgery .

450
01:05:49,647 --> 01:05:59,222
Hypoparathyroidism is characterized by facial muscle spasms, muscle cramps, and paresthesias of the lips, tongue, fingers, and feet.

451
01:05:59,222 --> 01:06:02,372
This can progress to tetany and seizures .

452
01:06:02,372 --> 01:06:12,122
Chronically, hypocalcemic pregnant women may also have a fetus with skeletal demineralization resulting in multiple bone fractures in the neonatal period .

453
01:06:12,122 --> 01:06:28,547
Maternal treatment includes calcitriol, dihydrotachysterol, or large vitamin D doses of 50,000 to 150,000 U/d; calcium gluconate or calcium lactate in doses of 3 to 5 g/d; and a low-phosphate diet.

454
01:06:28,547 --> 01:06:33,447
Fetal risks from large doses of vitamin D have not been established.

455
01:06:33,447 --> 01:06:41,109
During treatment, the therapeutic challenge in women with known hypoparathyroidism is management of blood calcium levels.

456
01:06:41,109 --> 01:06:51,334
It is possible that the greater calcium absorption typical of pregnancy will result in lower calcium requirements or that the fetal demand for calcium will result in greater need.

457
01:06:51,334 --> 01:06:57,147
The goal during pregnancy is to maintain a corrected calcium level in the low normal range.

458
01:06:57,147 --> 01:07:01,872
# Pregnancy and Lactation-associated Osteoporosis.

459
01:07:01,872 --> 01:07:10,584
In most gravidas, even with their remarkably increased calcium requirements, it is uncertain whether pregnancy causes osteopenia.

460
01:07:10,584 --> 01:07:15,809
In one study of 200 women, bone density declined during pregnancy .

461
01:07:15,809 --> 01:07:23,159
Women who breastfed, carried twin pregnancies, or had a low body mass index were at higher risk of bone loss.

462
01:07:23,159 --> 01:07:32,309
Thomas and Weisman (2006) in their review cite a 3- to 4-percent average reduction in bone mineral density during pregnancy.

463
01:07:32,309 --> 01:07:39,909
Lactation also represents a period of negative calcium balance that may be corrected through maternal skeletal resorption.

464
01:07:39,909 --> 01:07:54,584
However, data from the Women’s Health Initiative found that parity, number of pregnancies, age at first pregnancy, breastfeeding, and duration of breastfeeding were not associated with postmenopausal hip fracture risk or bone density losses .

465
01:07:54,584 --> 01:08:02,959
The most common symptom of this rare disorder of pregnancy-associated osteoporosis is back pain in late pregnancy or postpartum.

466
01:08:02,959 --> 01:08:11,109
Other symptoms are hip pain, either unilateral or bilateral, and difficulty in weight bearing until the woman is nearly immobilized .

467
01:08:11,109 --> 01:08:16,272
In more than half of women, no apparent reason for osteopenia is found.

468
01:08:16,272 --> 01:08:27,284
Some known causes are heparin (unfractionated only), bed rest, and corticosteroid therapy (Cunningham, 2005; Galambosi, 2016).

469
01:08:27,284 --> 01:08:33,922
In a few cases, overt hyperparathyroidism or thyrotoxicosis eventually develops.

470
01:08:33,922 --> 01:08:40,259
Treatment is problematic and includes calcium and vitamin D supplementation and pain management.

471
01:08:40,259 --> 01:08:41,947
Long-term

472
01:08:42,100 --> 01:08:50,800
surveillance of osteopenia indicates that although bone density improves, these women and their offspring may have chronic osteopenia .

473
01:08:50,750 --> 01:09:03,925
Related, prenatal supplementation of normal women with cholecalciferol, 1000 IU/d, did not increase offspring bone mineral content, although it did ensure maternal vitamin D repletion .

474
01:09:03,925 --> 01:09:09,525
In lactating women with osteoporosis, some advocate cessation of breastfeeding .

475
01:09:09,525 --> 01:09:11,987
Adrenal Gland Disorders.

476
01:09:11,987 --> 01:09:14,737
# Pheochromocytoma.

477
01:09:14,737 --> 01:09:19,062
Pregnancy has profound effects on adrenal cortical secretion.

478
01:09:19,062 --> 01:09:28,225
These interrelationships were reviewed by Lekarev and New (2011) and are discussed in detail in Chapter 4 (p. 73).

479
01:09:28,225 --> 01:09:32,250
Several adrenal disorders may be encountered in pregnancy.

480
01:09:32,250 --> 01:09:43,587
Of these, pheochromocytomas are chromaffin tumors that secrete catecholamines and usually are located in the adrenal medulla, although 10 percent are located in sympathetic ganglia.

481
01:09:43,587 --> 01:09:52,700
They are called the 10-percent tumor because approximately 10 percent are bilateral, 10 percent are extra adrenal, and 10 percent are malignant.

482
01:09:52,700 --> 01:10:07,862
These tumors can be associated with medullary thyroid carcinoma and hyperparathyroidism in some of the autosomally dominant or recessive multiple endocrine neoplasia syndromes, as well as in neurofibromatosis and von Hippel-Lindau disease .

483
01:10:07,862 --> 01:10:13,537
Pheochromocytomas complicate approximately 1 per 50,000 pregnancies .

484
01:10:13,537 --> 01:10:19,887
Notably, they are found in 0.2 to 0.6 percent of hypertensive patients .

485
01:10:19,887 --> 01:10:27,262
Symptoms are usually paroxysmal and manifest as hypertensive crisis, seizures, or anxiety attacks.

486
01:10:27,262 --> 01:10:34,512
Others are headaches, sweating, palpitations, chest pain, nausea, vomiting, and pallor or flushing.

487
01:10:34,512 --> 01:10:41,350
Hypertension is sustained in 60 percent of patients, but half of these also have paroxysmal crises.

488
01:10:41,350 --> 01:10:49,212
Standard screening is quantification of metanephrines and catecholamine metabolites in a 24-hour urine specimen .

489
01:10:49,212 --> 01:11:01,275
Diagnosis is established by measurement of a 24-hour urine collection with at least two of three assays for free catecholamines, metanephrines, or vanillylmandelic acid (VMA).

490
01:11:01,275 --> 01:11:11,537
Determination of plasma catecholamine metabolite levels is the most sensitive test, and a pheochromocytoma diagnosis is excluded if they are within normal range .

491
01:11:11,537 --> 01:11:21,650
In nonpregnant patients, adrenal localization is usually successful with either computed tomography (CT) or magnetic resonance (MR) imaging.

492
01:11:21,650 --> 01:11:26,537
For most cases, preferred treatment is laparoscopic adrenalectomy .

493
01:11:26,537 --> 01:11:31,937
In pregnancy, these tumors are rare but result in dangerous complications.

494
01:11:31,937 --> 01:11:39,450
Geelhoed (1983) provided an earlier review of 89 cases in which 43 mothers died.

495
01:11:39,450 --> 01:11:46,837
Maternal death was much more common if the tumor was not diagnosed antepartum—58 versus 18 percent.

496
01:11:46,837 --> 01:11:53,575
As seen in Table 61-5, maternal mortality rates are now lower but still formidable.

497
01:11:53,575 --> 01:12:01,800
In one review of 77 cases, antepartum diagnosis was the most important determinant of maternal mortality risk .

498
01:12:01,800 --> 01:12:09,900
However, Salazar-Vega and colleagues (2014) described good outcomes in women diagnosed after delivery.

499
01:12:09,900 --> 01:12:12,637
Table 61-5.

500
01:12:12,637 --> 01:12:24,287
Outcomes of Pregnancies Complicated by Pheochromocytoma and Reported in Four Contiguous Epochs Diagnosis of pheochromocytoma in pregnancy is similar to that for nonpregnant patients.

501
01:12:24,287 --> 01:12:30,700
MR imaging is the preferred technique to locate adrenal and extra-adrenal pheochromocytomas .

502
01:12:30,700 --> 01:12:38,362
Importantly, differentiating preeclampsia from the hypertensive crisis caused by pheochromocytoma is essential.

503
01:12:38,362 --> 01:12:41,125
Figure 61-5.

504
01:12:41,125 --> 01:12:52,562
Coronal magnetic resonance image taken in a 32-week pregnant woman shows a rightsided pheochromocytoma (arrow) and its position relative to the liver (L) above it.

505
01:12:52,562 --> 01:12:59,837
Immediate control of hypertension and symptoms is attained with an αadrenergic blocking agent such as phenoxybenzamine.

506
01:12:59,837 --> 01:13:04,337
The dose is 10 to 30 mg, two to four times daily.

507
01:13:04,337 --> 01:13:11,225
After α-blockade is achieved, β-blocking drugs such as propranolol may be given for tachycardia.

508
01:13:11,225 --> 01:13:23,550
In many cases, surgical exploration and tumor removal are performed during pregnancy, preferably during the second trimester (Biggar, 2013; Dong, 2014).

509
01:13:23,550 --> 01:13:32,612
Successful laparoscopic removal of adrenal tumors has become the norm (Miller, 2012; Zuluaga-Gómez, 2012).

510
01:13:32,612 --> 01:13:38,212
Donatini and colleagues (2018) described three cases of women with

511
01:13:38,380 --> 01:13:52,017
bilateral tumors who had laparoscopic unilateral adrenalectomy of the largest tumor during pregnancy and subsequent contralateral adrenalectomy after delivery to avoid the maternal and fetal effects of adrenal insufficiency.

512
01:13:51,967 --> 01:13:59,967
If diagnosed later in pregnancy, either planned cesarean delivery with tumor excision or postpartum resection is appropriate.

513
01:13:59,967 --> 01:14:07,792
Recurrent tumors are troublesome, and even with good blood pressure control, dangerous peripartum hypertension may develop.

514
01:14:07,792 --> 01:14:14,042
We have cared for three women in whom recurrent pheochromocytoma was identified during pregnancy.

515
01:14:14,042 --> 01:14:18,355
Hypertension was managed with phenoxybenzamine in all three.

516
01:14:18,355 --> 01:14:27,855
Two newborns were healthy, but a third was stillborn in a mother with a massive tumor burden who was receiving phenoxybenzamine, 100 mg daily.

517
01:14:27,855 --> 01:14:31,892
In all three women, the tumor was resected postpartum.

518
01:14:31,892 --> 01:14:34,380
# Cushing Syndrome.

519
01:14:34,380 --> 01:14:39,055
This syndrome is rare and the female:male ratio is 3:1 .

520
01:14:39,055 --> 01:14:45,017
Most cases of Cushing syndrome are iatrogenic from long-term corticosteroid treatment.

521
01:14:45,017 --> 01:14:53,755
Excessive glucocorticoids can also be the result of a pituitary adenoma, adrenal tumor, or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia .

522
01:14:53,755 --> 01:15:01,667
Cushing disease refers to bilateral adrenal hyperplasia stimulated by corticotropin-producing pituitary adenomas.

523
01:15:01,667 --> 01:15:07,092
Corticotropin is also called adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH).

524
01:15:07,092 --> 01:15:21,655
Most adenomas are microadenomas measuring <1 cm, and half measure <5 mm. Rarely, abnormal secretion of hypothalamic corticotropin-releasing factor may cause corticotropic hyperplasia.

525
01:15:21,655 --> 01:15:31,492
Such hyperplasia may also be caused by nonendocrine tumors that produce polypeptides similar to either corticotropin-releasing factor or corticotropin.

526
01:15:31,492 --> 01:15:39,705
Less than a fourth of cases of Cushing syndrome are corticotropin independent, and most of these are caused by an adrenal adenoma .

527
01:15:39,705 --> 01:15:43,680
Tumors are usually bilateral, and half are malignant.

528
01:15:43,680 --> 01:15:49,242
Occasionally, associated androgen excess may lead to severe virilization.

529
01:15:49,242 --> 01:15:58,380
The typical cushingoid body habitus is caused by adipose tissue deposition creating moon facies, a buffalo hump, and truncal obesity.

530
01:15:58,380 --> 01:16:11,805
Fatigability and weakness, hypertension, hirsutism, and amenorrhea are each encountered in 75 to 85 percent of nonpregnant patients (Hatipoglu, 2012; Melmed, 2020).

531
01:16:11,805 --> 01:16:17,030
Personality changes, easy bruising, and cutaneous striae are common.

532
01:16:17,030 --> 01:16:21,180
Up to 60 percent may have impaired glucose tolerance.

533
01:16:21,180 --> 01:16:37,892
Diagnosis can be difficult and is suggested by elevated plasma cortisol levels that cannot be suppressed by dexamethasone or by elevated 24-hour urinary free cortisol excretion in three collections (Arit, 2018; Loriaux, 2017).

534
01:16:37,892 --> 01:16:44,117
Neither test is totally accurate, and each is more difficult to interpret in obese patients.

535
01:16:44,117 --> 01:16:52,705
Serum corticotropin levels and CT and MR imaging are used to localize pituitary and adrenal tumors or hyperplasia.

536
01:16:52,705 --> 01:17:03,167
In pregnancy, diagnosis can be difficult because of pregnancy-induced increases in plasma cortisol, corticotropin, and corticotropin-releasing factor levels.

537
01:17:03,167 --> 01:17:09,767
Measurement of urinary free cortisol excretion in a 24-hour collection sample is recommended.

538
01:17:09,767 --> 01:17:15,467
With interpretation of results, the normal elevation seen in pregnancy is factored.

539
01:17:15,467 --> 01:17:24,717
Because most women have corticotropindependent Cushing syndrome, associated androgen excess may cause anovulation, and pregnancy is rare.

540
01:17:24,717 --> 01:17:35,030
Caimari and coworkers (2017) identified 263 pregnancies in 220 women with Cushing syndrome in their systematic review.

541
01:17:35,030 --> 01:17:48,705
These differ compared with nonpregnant women in that more than half are caused by corticotropin-independent adrenal adenomas (Kamoun, 2014; Lacroix, 2015; Machado, 2018).

542
01:17:48,705 --> 01:17:56,242
Approximately 30 percent of cases are from a pituitary adenoma, and 10 percent from adrenal carcinomas.

543
01:17:56,242 --> 01:18:05,905
Pregnancy outcomes reviewed by Caimari and associates (2017) in women with Cushing syndrome are listed in Table 61-6.

544
01:18:05,905 --> 01:18:08,617
Table 61-6.

545
01:18:08,617 --> 01:18:28,817
Complications in Pregnancies of Women with Active and Cured Cushing Syndrome Long-term medical therapy for Cushing syndrome usually is ineffective, and definitive therapy is resection of the pituitary or adrenal adenoma or bilateral adrenalectomy for hyperplasia (Lacroix, 2015; Machado, 2018).

546
01:18:28,817 --> 01:18:32,430
During pregnancy, management of hypertension

547
01:18:32,596 --> 01:18:36,083
in mild cases may suffice until delivery.

548
01:18:36,033 --> 01:18:44,646
In their review, Lindsay and associates (2005) described primary medical therapy in 20 women with Cushing syndrome.

549
01:18:44,646 --> 01:18:51,658
Most were successfully treated with metyrapone as an interim treatment until definitive surgery after delivery.

550
01:18:51,658 --> 01:18:55,296
A few cases were treated with oral ketoconazole.

551
01:18:55,296 --> 01:19:03,546
However, because this drug also blocks testicular steroidogenesis, treatment during pregnancy with a male fetus is worrisome.

552
01:19:03,546 --> 01:19:14,633
Mifepristone, the norethindrone derivative used for abortion and labor induction, has shown promise for treating Cushing disease but should not be used in pregnancy for obvious reasons.

553
01:19:14,633 --> 01:19:27,058
Transsphenoidal resection of a pituitary mass or laparoscopic adrenalectomy are considered first-line treatment options in the second trimester (Brue, 2018; Manoharan, 2020).

554
01:19:27,058 --> 01:19:34,033
Unilateral adrenalectomy has been safely performed in the early third trimester and can also be curative .

555
01:19:34,033 --> 01:19:38,096
# Adrenal Insufficiency—Addison Disease.

556
01:19:38,096 --> 01:19:46,958
Primary adrenocortical insufficiency is rare because more than 90 percent of total gland volume must be destroyed for symptoms to develop.

557
01:19:46,958 --> 01:20:01,721
Autoimmune adrenalitis is the most common cause in the developed world, but tuberculosis is a more frequent etiology in resource-poor countries (Arit, 2018; Betterle, 2019; Kamoun, 2014).

558
01:20:01,721 --> 01:20:09,433
In the United States, the prevalence in 7.7 million births was 1 case per 10,000 to 20,000 .

559
01:20:09,433 --> 01:20:19,271
There is an increased incidence of concurrent Hashimoto thyroiditis, primary ovarian insufficiency, type 1 diabetes, and Graves disease.

560
01:20:19,271 --> 01:20:29,346
These polyglandular autoimmune syndromes also include pernicious anemia, vitiligo, alopecia, nontropical sprue, and myasthenia gravis.

561
01:20:29,346 --> 01:20:37,271
Untreated adrenal hypofunction frequently causes infertility, but with replacement therapy, ovulation is restored.

562
01:20:37,271 --> 01:20:43,046
If untreated, women often note weakness, nausea, vomiting, and weight loss.

563
01:20:43,046 --> 01:20:49,896
Low levels of plasma cortisol and elevated ACTH levels are generally considered diagnostic .

564
01:20:49,896 --> 01:21:03,296
Because serum cortisol levels rise during pregnancy, a short ACTHstimulation test documenting less than a twofold increase in plasma cortisol in response to infused corticotropin confirms the diagnosis .

565
01:21:03,296 --> 01:21:17,033
In a large cohort study, 1188 women with Addison disease were compared with more than 11,000 age-matched controls who delivered between 1973 and 2006 (Björnsdottir, 2010).

566
01:21:17,033 --> 01:21:29,033
Women diagnosed with adrenal insufficiency within 3 years of delivery were significantly more likely to deliver preterm, to deliver a low-birthweight newborn, and to undergo cesarean delivery.

567
01:21:29,033 --> 01:21:32,446
Others have reported similar adverse outcomes .

568
01:21:32,446 --> 01:21:39,933
Most pregnant women with Addison disease are already taking glucocorticoid and mineralocorticoid replacement drugs.

569
01:21:39,933 --> 01:21:47,446
These should be continued, and women are observed for evidence of either inadequate or excessive corticosteroid replacement.

570
01:21:47,446 --> 01:21:56,371
A 20- to 40-percent dose increase is usually expected after midpregnancy to mimic the physiological cortisol elevation in pregnancy .

571
01:21:56,371 --> 01:22:07,608
During labor, delivery, and postpartum, or after a surgical procedure, corticosteroid replacement must be increased appreciably to approximate the normal adrenal response.

572
01:22:07,608 --> 01:22:17,321
This augmented dose is called a stress dose, and one option is hydrocortisone, 100 mg, given IV every 8 hours for 48 hours.

573
01:22:17,321 --> 01:22:27,408
Importantly, shock from causes other than adrenocortical insufficiency—for example, hemorrhage or sepsis—must be recognized and treated promptly.

574
01:22:27,408 --> 01:22:30,546
# Primary Aldosteronism.

575
01:22:30,546 --> 01:22:38,646
Hyperaldosteronism is caused by an adrenal adenoma—Conn syndrome—in approximately 75 percent of cases.

576
01:22:38,646 --> 01:22:49,883
Idiopathic bilateral adrenal hyperplasia makes up the remainder, except for rare cases of adrenal carcinoma (Eschler, 2015; Yang, 2020).

577
01:22:49,883 --> 01:22:54,846
Findings include hypertension, hypokalemia, and muscle weakness.

578
01:22:54,846 --> 01:22:59,646
High serum or urine levels of aldosterone confirm the diagnosis.

579
01:22:59,646 --> 01:23:12,883
In normal pregnancy, as discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 74), progesterone blocks aldosterone action, and this results in very high aldosterone levels (Appendix, p. 1230).

580
01:23:12,883 --> 01:23:18,571
Accordingly, the diagnosis of hyperaldosteronism during pregnancy can be difficult.

581
01:23:18,571 --> 01:23:22,446
Because renin levels are suppressed in pregnant women with

582
01:23:22,612 --> 01:23:29,649
hyperaldosteronism, a plasma aldosterone-to-renin activity ratio may be helpful for diagnosis .

583
01:23:29,599 --> 01:23:38,562
Hypertension worsens as pregnancy progresses, and medical management includes potassium supplementation and antihypertensive therapy.

584
01:23:38,562 --> 01:23:43,087
In many cases, hypertension responds to spironolactone.

585
01:23:43,087 --> 01:23:52,537
However, β-blocking or calcium channel–blocking drugs may be preferred because of the potential fetal antiandrogenic effects of spironolactone.

586
01:23:52,537 --> 01:23:59,399
Mascetti and coworkers (2011) reported successful use of amiloride in a pregnant woman.

587
01:23:59,399 --> 01:24:05,687
Use of eplerenone, a selective aldosterone-receptor antagonist, also has been described .

588
01:24:05,687 --> 01:24:18,674
Laparoscopic tumor resection is considered curative, but the optimal management during pregnancy, either surgical or medical, remains unclear (Eschler, 2015; Morton, 2015).

589
01:24:18,674 --> 01:24:20,999
Pituitary Disorders.

590
01:24:20,999 --> 01:24:23,612
# Prolactinomas.

591
01:24:23,612 --> 01:24:32,299
The pituitary enlarges impressively during pregnancy, predominantly from lactotrophic cellular hyperplasia induced by estrogen .

592
01:24:32,299 --> 01:24:36,749
Several pituitary disorders also can complicate pregnancy.

593
01:24:36,749 --> 01:24:44,037
Of these, prolactinomas are tumors that constitute 75 percent of pituitary adenomas in women .

594
01:24:44,037 --> 01:24:50,574
They are often found in nonpregnant women since the advent of widely available serum prolactin assays.

595
01:24:50,574 --> 01:24:56,974
Serum levels <25 pg/mL are considered normal in nonpregnant women.

596
01:24:56,974 --> 01:25:03,549
Adenoma symptoms and findings include amenorrhea, galactorrhea, and hyperprolactinemia.

597
01:25:03,549 --> 01:25:09,462
Tumors are classified arbitrarily by their size measured by CT or MR imaging.

598
01:25:09,462 --> 01:25:26,124
A microadenoma is ≤10 mm, and a macroadenoma is >10 mm. Treatment for microadenomas is usually with bromocriptine, a dopamine agonist and powerful prolactin inhibitor, which frequently restores ovulation.

599
01:25:26,124 --> 01:25:36,787
For suprasellar macroadenomas, most recommend surgical resection before pregnancy is attempted (Araujo, 2015; Melmed, 2020).

600
01:25:36,787 --> 01:25:48,174
In a pooled analysis of more than 750 pregnant women with prolactinomas, only 2.4 percent with microadenomas developed symptomatic enlargement during pregnancy .

601
01:25:48,174 --> 01:25:57,249
Symptomatic enlargement of macroadenomas, however, is more frequent and was found in 21 percent of 238 pregnant women.

602
01:25:57,249 --> 01:26:04,912
In another study of 46 women with a macroprolactinoma, 20 percent had tumor growth–related symptoms .

603
01:26:04,912 --> 01:26:11,012
Nonfunctioning adenomas can also cause symptoms of pituitary expansion in pregnancy .

604
01:26:11,012 --> 01:26:17,237
Pregnant women with microadenomas should be queried regularly for headaches and visual symptoms.

605
01:26:17,237 --> 01:26:23,574
Those with macroadenomas are followed more closely and have visual field testing each trimester.

606
01:26:23,574 --> 01:26:28,474
CT or MR imaging is recommended only if symptoms develop .

607
01:26:28,474 --> 01:26:36,749
Serial serum prolactin levels serve little use because of normal rises during pregnancy (Appendix, p. 1231).

608
01:26:36,749 --> 01:26:42,324
Symptomatic tumor enlargement should be treated immediately with a dopamine antagonist.

609
01:26:42,324 --> 01:26:46,712
The safety of bromocriptine in pregnancy is well established.

610
01:26:46,712 --> 01:26:50,612
The safety profile is less well known for cabergoline.

611
01:26:50,612 --> 01:26:56,912
This drug is increasingly used in nonpregnant women because it is better tolerated and more effective.

612
01:26:56,912 --> 01:27:04,962
Cabergoline is generally considered safe for use in pregnancy (Huang, 2019; O’Sullivan, 2020).

613
01:27:04,962 --> 01:27:11,612
Surgery is recommended for women with no response, including those with pituitary apoplexy .

614
01:27:11,612 --> 01:27:14,387
Figure 61-6.

615
01:27:14,387 --> 01:27:18,574
Magnetic resonance imaging of a pituitary adenoma.

616
01:27:18,574 --> 01:27:26,037
This sagittal T1-weighed image demonstrates a fluid-fluid level, consistent with intratumoral hemorrhage (arrow).

617
01:27:26,037 --> 01:27:29,774
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Toral Patel.

618
01:27:29,774 --> 01:27:32,137
# Acromegaly.

619
01:27:32,137 --> 01:27:39,662
This is caused by excessive growth hormone, usually from an acidophilic or a chromophobic pituitary adenoma.

620
01:27:39,662 --> 01:27:46,324
In normal pregnancy, pituitary growth hormone levels decline as placental epitopes are secreted.

621
01:27:46,324 --> 01:27:53,399
Diagnosis is suggested by elevated insulin growth factor 1 (IGF-1) serum levels.

622
01:27:53,399 --> 01:28:05,862
Levels are more indicative in the first half of pregnancy because IGF-1 concentrations rise in normal pregnancy after midpregnancy (Abucham, 2017; Melmed, 2020).

623
01:28:05,862 --> 01:28:10,537
Definitive diagnosis may best be postponed until after delivery.

624
01:28:10,537 --> 01:28:19,612
Fewer than 100 cases of acromegaly have been reported during pregnancy (Cheng, 2012; Dias, 2013).

625
01:28:19,612 --> 01:28:25,999
Pregnancy is rare in women with acromegaly because half are hyperprolactinemic and anovulatory.

626
01:28:25,999 --> 01:28:28,949
During pregnancy, affected women

627
01:28:29,116 --> 01:28:37,878
are at marginally greater risk for gestational diabetes and hypertension (Dias, 2013; Jallad, 2018).

628
01:28:37,828 --> 01:28:44,691
Management is similar to that for prolactinomas, and women are monitored for symptoms of tumor enlargement.

629
01:28:44,691 --> 01:28:49,466
Dopamine agonist therapy is less effective than for prolactinomas.

630
01:28:49,466 --> 01:28:59,503
Transsphenoidal resection, generally considered first-line treatment outside of pregnancy, may be necessary for symptomatic tumor enlargement during pregnancy .

631
01:28:59,503 --> 01:29:11,978
Guven and associates (2006) reported a case of pituitary apoplexy necessitating emergent transsphenoidal adenoma resection and cesarean delivery at 34 weeks’ gestation.

632
01:29:11,978 --> 01:29:25,028
Successful treatment of pregnant women with the somatostatin-receptor ligand octreotide and with the growth hormone analogue pegvisomant has been reported (Dias, 2013; Fleseriu, 2015).

633
01:29:25,028 --> 01:29:27,966
# Diabetes Insipidus.

634
01:29:27,966 --> 01:29:36,928
Vasopressin deficiency evident in diabetes insipidus (DI) is most commonly due to neurohypophysis agenesis or destruction.

635
01:29:36,928 --> 01:29:40,278
True DI rarely complicates pregnancy.

636
01:29:40,278 --> 01:29:49,178
Instead, other types of DI encountered during pregnancy include primary polydipsia, gestational DI, and nephrogenic DI.

637
01:29:49,178 --> 01:29:57,078
These show suppressed secretion of, increased degradation of, and renal insensitivity to vasopressin, respectively .

638
01:29:57,078 --> 01:30:04,528
In our experiences, transient secondary DI is more likely encountered with acute fatty liver of pregnancy .

639
01:30:04,528 --> 01:30:10,166
This probably stems from altered vasopressinase clearance because of hepatic dysfunction .

640
01:30:10,166 --> 01:30:20,628
DI therapy is intranasal administration of a synthetic analogue of vasopressin, desmopressin, which is 1-deamino-8-D-arginine vasopressin (DDAVP).

641
01:30:20,628 --> 01:30:25,391
Available data on the safety of DDAVP during pregnancy is reassuring .

642
01:30:25,391 --> 01:30:34,066
Most women require higher doses during pregnancy because of an increased metabolic clearance rate stimulated by placental vasopressinase .

643
01:30:34,066 --> 01:30:42,128
The prevalence of placental vasopressinase-induced DI is estimated at 2 to 4 cases per 100,000 pregnancies .

644
01:30:42,128 --> 01:30:44,553
# Sheehan Syndrome.

645
01:30:44,553 --> 01:30:54,203
Sheehan (1937) reported that pituitary ischemia and necrosis associated with obstetrical blood loss could result in hypopituitarism.

646
01:30:54,203 --> 01:31:04,828
With modern methods of hemorrhagic shock treatment, Sheehan syndrome rates have gradually declined (Matsuzaki, 2017; Pappachan, 2015).

647
01:31:04,828 --> 01:31:12,153
Affected women may have persistent hypotension, tachycardia, hypoglycemia, and lactation failure.

648
01:31:12,153 --> 01:31:23,166
Because deficiencies of some or all pituitary-responsive hormones may develop after the initial insult, Sheehan syndrome can be heterogenous and may not be identified for years .

649
01:31:23,166 --> 01:31:30,341
In one cohort study of 60 women with Sheehan syndrome, the average time to diagnosis was 13 years .

650
01:31:30,341 --> 01:31:37,728
Because adrenal insufficiency is the most lifethreatening complication, adrenal function should be immediately assessed.

651
01:31:37,728 --> 01:31:46,316
After glucocorticoid replacement, subsequent analyses and replacement of thyroid, gonadal, and growth hormones is considered.

652
01:31:46,316 --> 01:31:50,816
Women with Sheehan syndrome who become pregnant likely have a livebirth .

653
01:31:50,816 --> 01:31:54,003
# Lymphocytic Hypophysitis.

654
01:31:54,003 --> 01:32:03,753
This rare autoimmune pituitary disorder is characterized by massive infiltration by lymphocytes and plasma cells with parenchymal destruction of the gland.

655
01:32:03,753 --> 01:32:12,691
Many cases are temporally linked to pregnancy, and more than half of reported cases in women are identified during pregnancy or puerperium .

656
01:32:12,691 --> 01:32:18,316
However, more recent reports describe fewer cases associated with pregnancy .

657
01:32:18,316 --> 01:32:26,066
The degrees of hypopituitarism or mass effect symptoms, including headaches and visual field defects, vary.

658
01:32:26,066 --> 01:32:29,966
A sellar mass is seen with CT or MR imaging.

659
01:32:29,966 --> 01:32:40,316
A mass accompanied by a modestly elevated serum prolactin level—usually <100 pg/mL—suggests lymphocytic hypophysitis.

660
01:32:40,316 --> 01:32:46,941
In contrast, levels >200 pg/mL are encountered with prolactinoma.

661
01:32:46,941 --> 01:33:00,166
The etiology is unknown, but nearly 30 percent have a history of coexisting autoimmune diseases including Hashimoto thyroiditis, Addison disease, type 1 diabetes, or pernicious anemia .

662
01:33:00,166 --> 01:33:04,941
Treatment is with glucocorticoids and pituitary hormone replacement.

663
01:33:04,941 --> 01:33:16,066
The disease may be self-limited, and a careful withdrawal of hormone replacement is attempted after inflammation subsides (Foyouzi, 2011; Melmed, 2020).

664
01:33:16,066 --> 01:33:20,503
More than two thirds of women who require treatment for lymphocytic

665
01:33:20,668 --> 01:33:25,830
hypophysitis, however, will require long-term hormone replacement .

